<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Idrimi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">15th century BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Idrimi was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alalakh">Alalakh</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amuq-valley">Amuq plain</a> in the 15th century BCE, a refugee prince from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> who took power with the help of Habiru warriors and ruled as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a>. His life story is inscribed on a seated statue found at Alalakh in 1939 and now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>. It is one of the few autobiographical texts of the period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/idrimi.jpg" alt="Idrimi" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · CC BY-SA 2.0 fr · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_King_Idrimi-IMG_4553-black.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
